1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,211
A ship radios the harbour: we are
five kilometres out, at thirty seven

2
00:00:05,211 --> 00:00:06,111
degrees.

3
00:00:05,957 --> 00:00:06,857
No east.

4
00:00:06,766 --> 00:00:07,666
No north.

5
00:00:07,628 --> 00:00:09,793
Just a distance and an angle.

6
00:00:10,193 --> 00:00:12,252
And that is enough to find the ship.

7
00:00:12,252 --> 00:00:14,505
Two numbers again, but a different pair.

8
00:00:14,955 --> 00:00:17,662
Aarav noticed the same
thing on his ride today.

9
00:00:18,012 --> 00:00:21,632
His map could say four
kilometres east and three north.

10
00:00:21,632 --> 00:00:25,816
Or it could say five kilometres,
at about thirty seven degrees.

11
00:00:25,816 --> 00:00:26,741
Same place.

12
00:00:27,141 --> 00:00:29,974
Complex numbers can be
written both ways too.

13
00:00:29,974 --> 00:00:31,581
Today: the polar form.

14
00:00:32,231 --> 00:00:33,410
A quick note.

15
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:36,960
The polar form is not in
the current NCERT text.

16
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:42,082
But the JEE Main syllabus lists the
modulus and the argument, and JEE uses

17
00:00:42,082 --> 00:00:43,938
this form all the time.

18
00:00:44,538 --> 00:00:46,430
Recall episode forty two.

19
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:51,940
z equals a plus i b has a modulus r,
how far, and an argument theta, which

20
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:52,840
way.

21
00:00:52,895 --> 00:00:53,795
Step one.

22
00:00:53,759 --> 00:00:56,814
Drop a line from P down to the real axis.

23
00:00:56,814 --> 00:01:00,449
You get a right angled
triangle, with long side r.

24
00:01:01,199 --> 00:01:02,142
Step two.

25
00:01:02,142 --> 00:01:03,510
The base is a.

26
00:01:03,510 --> 00:01:08,144
Cos theta is base over long
side, so a equals r cos theta.

27
00:01:08,894 --> 00:01:09,898
Step three.

28
00:01:09,898 --> 00:01:11,305
The height is b.

29
00:01:11,305 --> 00:01:15,811
Sin theta is height over long
side, so b equals r sin theta.

30
00:01:16,561 --> 00:01:17,534
Step four.

31
00:01:17,534 --> 00:01:20,030
Put both back into a plus i b.

32
00:01:20,030 --> 00:01:23,388
z equals r cos theta, plus i r sin theta.

33
00:01:24,138 --> 00:01:25,172
Take r out.

34
00:01:25,172 --> 00:01:28,548
z equals r, times cos
theta plus i sin theta.

35
00:01:28,548 --> 00:01:30,342
That is the polar form.

36
00:01:30,992 --> 00:01:32,428
Now watch it live.

37
00:01:32,428 --> 00:01:36,747
Theta counts up, and z swings
around O at a fixed distance r.

38
00:01:37,147 --> 00:01:41,556
Now r grows, and z slides
outward along the same direction.

39
00:01:41,556 --> 00:01:43,159
r controls how far.

40
00:01:43,159 --> 00:01:45,096
Theta controls which way.

41
00:01:45,496 --> 00:01:51,049
And the mirror, z bar, is r, times
cos of minus theta plus i sin of minus

42
00:01:51,049 --> 00:01:51,949
theta.

43
00:01:51,724 --> 00:01:53,648
Same r, opposite angle.

44
00:01:54,298 --> 00:01:55,216
Quick one.

45
00:01:55,216 --> 00:01:56,850
Add two pi to theta.

46
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:57,936
Does z move?

47
00:01:57,936 --> 00:01:59,233
Pause and think.

48
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:03,333
No!

49
00:02:02,891 --> 00:02:05,931
A full turn brings z
back to the same point.

50
00:02:05,931 --> 00:02:10,544
That is why we pick one value, the
principal argument, above minus pi,

51
00:02:10,544 --> 00:02:11,444
up to pi.

52
00:02:12,071 --> 00:02:13,180
Side by side.

53
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:16,622
Cartesian form, a plus
i b, says along and up.

54
00:02:16,622 --> 00:02:18,317
It is best for adding.

55
00:02:18,717 --> 00:02:21,270
Polar form says how far and which way.

56
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:23,114
It is best for multiplying.

57
00:02:23,714 --> 00:02:24,817
Here is why.

58
00:02:24,817 --> 00:02:29,607
Multiply two numbers in polar form,
and the compound angle formulas from

59
00:02:29,607 --> 00:02:32,028
episode thirty three do the work.

60
00:02:32,028 --> 00:02:34,782
The r's multiply, and the angles add.

61
00:02:35,182 --> 00:02:40,268
So multiplying by i, which is one
unit at ninety degrees, simply turns

62
00:02:40,268 --> 00:02:42,222
z through ninety degrees.

63
00:02:42,222 --> 00:02:43,122
Watch.

64
00:02:43,507 --> 00:02:44,734
Now you try.

65
00:02:44,734 --> 00:02:50,618
Is two, times cos sixty degrees minus
i sin sixty degrees, in polar form?

66
00:02:50,618 --> 00:02:52,143
Pause and decide.

67
00:02:55,343 --> 00:02:56,243
No!

68
00:02:55,851 --> 00:02:58,129
Polar form needs a plus sign.

69
00:02:58,129 --> 00:03:03,437
Write it as two, times cos of minus
sixty, plus i sin of minus sixty.

70
00:03:03,437 --> 00:03:05,935
The angle is minus sixty degrees.

71
00:03:06,585 --> 00:03:07,800
Remember this.

72
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:12,260
Every non zero z is r, times
cos theta plus i sin theta.

73
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:14,695
Now, one worked example.

74
00:03:15,045 --> 00:03:17,889
Write one plus root three i in polar form.

75
00:03:18,389 --> 00:03:19,289
Step one.

76
00:03:19,081 --> 00:03:21,878
r is the root of one
plus three, which is two.

77
00:03:22,628 --> 00:03:23,528
Step two.

78
00:03:23,396 --> 00:03:27,265
cos theta is one half, and sin
theta is root three over two.

79
00:03:28,015 --> 00:03:28,983
Step three.

80
00:03:28,983 --> 00:03:32,768
Both are positive, so theta
is in the first quadrant.

81
00:03:32,768 --> 00:03:34,450
Theta is pi by three.

82
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:40,041
So z equals two, times cos pi
by three plus i sin pi by three.

83
00:03:40,691 --> 00:03:42,156
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:42,506 --> 00:03:47,107
Is minus two, times cos theta
plus i sin theta, in polar form?

85
00:03:47,107 --> 00:03:50,720
Many students say yes,
with r equal to minus two.

86
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:54,483
But r is a distance, so
it is never negative.

87
00:03:54,483 --> 00:03:59,695
Turn the angle by pi instead: two,
times cos of theta plus pi, plus i sin

88
00:03:59,695 --> 00:04:01,104
of theta plus pi.

89
00:04:01,754 --> 00:04:03,478
Where is this asked?

90
00:04:03,478 --> 00:04:08,664
In JEE Main and JEE Advanced, as
powers and products of complex numbers

91
00:04:08,664 --> 00:04:11,350
that are easy only in polar form.

92
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:13,153
Exam level now.

93
00:04:13,153 --> 00:04:15,935
Which is the polar form of this product?

94
00:04:15,935 --> 00:04:17,188
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:22,588 --> 00:04:23,949
The answer is A.

96
00:04:24,449 --> 00:04:28,661
One plus i has r equal to root
two, and angle pi by four.

97
00:04:29,161 --> 00:04:33,646
Root three plus i has r equal
to two, and angle pi by six.

98
00:04:34,146 --> 00:04:36,550
Multiply the r's: two root two.

99
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:41,337
Add the angles: pi by four plus
pi by six is five pi by twelve.

100
00:04:41,937 --> 00:04:43,396
Here is your recap.

101
00:04:43,396 --> 00:04:44,895
Screenshot this card.

102
00:04:48,095 --> 00:04:50,932
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

103
00:04:51,332 --> 00:04:52,232
One.

104
00:04:51,890 --> 00:04:54,201
Write minus one in polar form.

105
00:04:55,101 --> 00:04:56,001
Two.

106
00:04:55,607 --> 00:04:57,967
What are r and theta for three i?

107
00:04:58,867 --> 00:04:59,767
Three.

108
00:04:59,562 --> 00:05:01,850
Why can r never be negative?

109
00:05:02,950 --> 00:05:07,735
Next time: a ball thrown up reaches
some heights twice, and some never.

110
00:05:07,735 --> 00:05:10,808
Quadratic equations with
real and complex roots.
